## Level I status

**STILL OPEN.** The global theorem is still the literal every-minimizer statement
in `docs/problem/statement.md`. The finite source below is provably nonglobal.

## Starting endpoint and search choices

The run seeks a strict-sub-`D(n)` configuration with at least the full source
cardinality, if a sufficiently large triangle-free global source exists.
The canonical triangle-free lattice competitor regression, Audit.245, rules out
using triangle-freeness alone to suppress the known `D^(2/3)` packing reserve.
The C-I/positive-core rigidity stack also does not construct a nonlinear endpoint.

The chosen implication chain is

```text
source-adapted mixed replacement with exchange score >= 0
 -> every lower pair and every upper pair checked at a target d < D
 -> at least n retained/inserted labels
 -> strict-sub-D same-cardinality competitor
 -> contradiction to global minimality.
```

This deliberately permits replacement of a macroscopic fraction of the source,
changes in contact topology, and points from independent comparison packings.
There is no identification of a comparison packing with the source and no
requirement that an interpolation between their labels stay feasible.

Other ideas considered at this checkpoint were first-order affine/conformal
compression, variable-radius packing rigidity, and convergence to triangular
lattice bulk. None currently supplies the strict same-cardinality endpoint:
stationarity obstructs the first, the second changes the equal-radius model,
and the third gives approximate contacts rather than an exact unit triangle.
These are exploratory observations, not new exclusion theorems.

## 1. Exact collective exchange theorem

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion.

Let `A` be any finite unit-separated `n`-point source, with diameter `D`.
Fix `1 <= d < D` and a finite unit-separated comparison packing `X` with
`diam(X) <= d`. Labels in the two sets are distinct even if coordinates coincide.

On `A`, let `F_d` contain exactly the pairs at distance **greater than** `d`.
On the two label classes `A,X`, form a bipartite conflict graph `B_d` whose
edges are exactly the cross pairs at distance **less than** one or **greater
than** `d`. Unit ties and target-diameter ties are allowed. Coincident labels
conflict because their distance is zero.

For `Y subset X`, put `N(Y)=N_{B_d}(Y) subset A`. Write `tau` for the minimum
vertex-cover size of a graph and define

\[
 \Phi_d(A,X)=\max_{Y\subseteq X}
 \left\{|Y|-|N(Y)|-\tau\bigl(F_d[A\setminus N(Y)]\bigr)\right\}.
\]

Then the largest unit-separated subset of the labelled union `A union X`
having diameter at most `d` has cardinality exactly

\[
 \boxed{n+\Phi_d(A,X).}
\tag{1}
\]

Consequently, `Phi_d(A,X)>=0` proves that `A` is not a global minimizer.
Every actual global minimizer instead satisfies

\[
 \boxed{
 |N(Y)|+\tau(F_d[A\setminus N(Y)])\ge |Y|+1
 \quad\hbox{for every }Y\subseteq X.
 }
\tag{2}
\]

These are finite statements with no contact-minimality, lattice, stress,
triangle-free, or contact-isolate assumptions.

### Proof

Fix the inserted label set `Y`. Every old label in `N(Y)` must be deleted.
Of the other old labels, the additional deletions must cover all edges of
`F_d[A\N(Y)]`; conversely, any such cover suffices. Old pairs already obey
the unit lower bound, inserted pairs obey both bounds by the hypothesis on
`X`, and all retained cross pairs obey both bounds by the definition of `B_d`.
Thus the maximum size with inserted set exactly `Y` is

\[
 n-|N(Y)|-\tau(F_d[A\setminus N(Y)])+|Y|.
\]

Maximizing gives (1). If this size is at least `n`, take any `n` points of
the resulting set. Its diameter is at most `d<D`, contradicting global
minimality. Since the score is an integer, its strict negativity is
equivalent to (2). This argument retains every original source label in
the deletion/retention count, including contact isolates.

## 2. Equivalent matching formulation

For any vertex cover `T` of `F_d`, the old labels `S=A\T` already meet both
pair bounds. Let `nu(B_d[S,X])` denote the maximum matching size in the
remaining bipartite conflict graph. The exact maximum cardinality in
`S union X` is

\[
 |S|+|X|-\nu(B_d[S,X]).
\]

Indeed, a valid subset is the complement of a vertex cover of this
bipartite graph; its minimum vertex-cover size equals its maximum matching
size. Therefore the strict competitor condition is

\[
 \boxed{|X|-\nu(B_d[A\setminus T,X])\ge |T|.}
\tag{3}
\]

This is a finite combinatorial reformulation, using the standard bipartite
matching/cover identity. It is not a new geometric Hall-capacity theorem.
For a global source, every such `T` instead satisfies

\[
 \nu(B_d[A\setminus T,X])\ge |X|-|T|+1.
\tag{4}
\]

Allowing every cover `T`, (3) is equivalent to (1). To see the converse
without assuming a particular cover is optimal, take the deleted old
labels of a feasible union subset. They cover `F_d`; apply the displayed
maximum-cardinality formula to that cover.

The new search uses the complete upper-conflict graph at `d`, which can
include old nondiameter pairs. Replacing it by the original actual
diameter graph without an explicit positive margin is invalid. Likewise,
dropping cross upper conflicts turns (3) into a statement about a different
packing problem.

## 3. Exact finite certificate

**Classification:** regression; not a global-minimizer example.

The source is the 81-point square patch

\[
 A=\{-4,-3,\ldots,4\}^2,
 \qquad \operatorname{diam}(A)^2=128.
\]

Use the rational unit basis

\[
 u=(1,0),\qquad v=(8/17,15/17).
\]

For a nonzero index difference `(i,j)`, its squared length is
`i^2+j^2+(16/17)ij`. If `ij>=0` it is at least one. If `ij<0`, writing
`a=|i|,b=|j|` gives

\[
 (a-b)^2+\frac{18}{17}ab\ge\frac{18}{17}>1.
\]

The only unit differences are therefore `(+/-1,0),(0,+/-1)`, and the unit
contact graph is bipartite. The finite cloud consists of the first 80
nonzero sites `iu+jv` in the radius-five disk, with the exact ordering
specified in the verifier. Retain the old source origin and delete its
other 80 labels. The resulting 81 points have all pair distances at least
one and squared diameter exactly 100. The strict squared-diameter margin
is 28. The endpoint remains triangle-free.

The cloud alone has only 80 labels: a comparison-only count would not pay
the 81-point source cardinality. The mixed certificate does pay it. This
simple fixture exercises the full replacement accounting; it does not
show that a similar exchange exists for an arbitrary triangle-free source.

## 4. Discovery and verification

`research/global_exchange_pool_2026_10_12.py` constructs the complete
forbidden-pair graph of a finite rational pool. Unlike (1), the tool also
allows proposals from several mutually incompatible packings: it includes
their internal conflicts explicitly. An optional binary MILP searches
for a large independent subset. Solver output is only a proposed selection;
the selected points are checked independently with exact rational arithmetic.
No numerical solver lower bound or assertion of optimality is admitted as a
mathematical certificate.

`research/verify_global_exchange_criterion_2026_10_12.py` independently
checks all 4,096 abstract graphs with three old labels, three comparison
labels, arbitrary old upper conflicts, and arbitrary cross conflicts.
The exact formula matches brute-force maximum subset cardinality in every
case. This finite enumeration checks the implementation; the proof of
(1) applies at all cardinalities independently of enumeration.

Two additional exact guards are binding:

- Keeping two points of the unit square and two remote comparison points
  passes every lower constraint and pays deletion of its two diagonal
  upper conflicts, but fails cross upper constraints. The verifier rejects it.
- An inserted point can clear every nonisolated point and collide with a
  retained contact isolate. The complete-pair graph includes that collision.

Commands:

```text
python3 research/verify_global_exchange_criterion_2026_10_12.py
python3 research/global_exchange_pool_2026_10_12.py research/certificates/global_exchange_square81_2026_10_12.json
```

The committed JSON records the exact coordinates, selected labels, and
diameter margin. It is sufficient for verification without SciPy.

## Dependencies, scope, and remaining obligation

The exchange theorem uses finite graph definitions and elementary
vertex-cover accounting; its matching formulation uses the standard
bipartite matching/cover identity. It does not depend on any unreviewed
geometric report or on ZR/ZF. Audit.245 is a canonical failure shield for
the choice of global search, not a premise in its proof. The public
[problem statement](https://www.erdosproblems.com/99) was checked; no new
literature theorem was imported.

**Promotion recommendation:** preserve as unreviewed research. No
canonical frontier or Level I status changes. The exact exchange criterion
and certificate verifier are infrastructure for the global endpoint,
not proof that the endpoint is always available.

The unresolved global obligation is to construct, for every sufficiently
large triangle-free canonical source, a finite comparison pool with a
certified unit-separated subset of cardinality at least `n` and diameter
strictly below `D(n)`. In the internally feasible comparison-cloud setting
this means proving `Phi_d(A,X)>=0` for some `d<D(n)`. No such universal
construction is asserted here.
